<h2>What does GREAT tell us about human-AI decision-making?</h2>

GREAT tests whether a multi-agent RAG system will improve answer accuracy and usability.
The task of green retrofitting planning is split into 4 functional AI agents in GREAT:
<ul>
<li>(1) Whole Life Carbon Assessment Agent: To conduct lifecycle carbon measurement</li>
<li>(2) Energy Efficiency Agent: To carry out compliance check against key energy efficiency standards, such as ASHRAE</li>
<li>(3) Cost Agent: To support cost, project phasing, and business case feasibility analysis</li>
<li>(4) Building Research Establishment Environmental Assessment Method (BREEAM) Agent: To provide guidance on achieving top BREEAM certifcation results for a green retrofit project</li>
</ul>


The evaluation includes three phases (see Figure 1):
<ul>
<li>(a) A pilot study with construction experts</li>
<li>(b) Quantitative benchmarking with a standard Question-and-Answer (QA) set</li>
<li>(c) Qualitative user study with construction practitioners</li>
</ul>

