<h2>RAG is NOT machine learning: wrong tools for wrong problems</h2>
<ul>
<li>Machine Learning: Predicting unknown answers from data and model training (probabilistic: 𝑥% accuracy)</li>
<li>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): Find the right answers from documents faithfully (binary: correct/wrong)</li>
</ul>

It is easy to mix up many AI concepts, and the most common misconception is positioning RAG as another type of machine learning model.
In fact, RAG is a retrieval-based system that uses a knowledge base, such as a database of green building codes and standards, 
to provide evidence-grounded answers and reduce hallucinations in LLMs.
As illustrated in Figure 2, after receiving a query from users, the LLMs retrieve the most relevant documents by vector embedding, chunking and indexing.
K-means clustering facilitates context prepartion when the LLMs are generating answers to the users.

It is not a machine learning model, but rather a tool that can be used in conjunction with machine learning models to improve their performance.
Answers already exist in the knowledge base, and RAG is used to retrieve them, rather than generating new answers from data and training a model to predict the <em>most probable answers.</em>

